{"id":"h093","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Your supervisor asks you to determine the enthalpy of a certain chemical reaction. Which would you do?\nIs the correct answer \"Use a solution calorimeter such as a coffee-cup calorimeter to measure the heat.\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"A coffee-cup calorimeter operates at constant atmospheric pressure, so measured heat equals ΔH.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"A coffee-cup calorimeter is simple, inexpensive, and directly measures heat of a solution reaction.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"It is the standard laboratory method for experimentally determining enthalpy of reaction.","confidence":0.7,"lean":0.7,"theta":1.0996,"phi":0.0},{"text":"It only works well for reactions in solution that go to completion quickly without significant gas evolution.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"For combustion or gas-phase reactions a bomb calorimeter is more appropriate and measures ΔU, which must be converted to ΔH.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Hess's law with tabulated formation enthalpies can give ΔH without an experiment.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question does not rule out the conditions under which a coffee-cup calorimeter works, so its limitations matter only for specific reactions.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.7,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The coffee-cup calorimeter is a correct and sensible answer, though the best choice depends on the specific reaction.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"